#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043dc448-2d64-5bd5-b613-9351f25fcbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(120-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445331} {:answer/id #uuid "a61a1a66-20a6-5535-8610-2d133482e2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(300-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445357} {:answer/id #uuid "b18a7bfd-9662-5019-81d6-5d34da173873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(500-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445358} {:answer/id #uuid "bb01bcbc-7723-5c54-bf42-c8d5e8f3b897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(70-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445360} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(250-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445365} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06901e9-40fe-5fc7-ae69-f932c626e4ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4108ce6a-5d28-540e-b8c0-e4b52161a458" :question/math-academy-id "q-344232" :question/problem "A parking garage initially contains $70$ vehicles. The number of vehicles in the garage $V(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in hours. If the maximum capacity of the garage is $250$ vehicles, which differential equation could be used to model the number of vehicles in the garage? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of vehicles in the garage obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of vehicles changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of vehicles in the garage. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-V),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the garage, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=250$ vehicles, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(250-V).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079c9887-f4cd-5533-8644-4c4f30e866d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$635$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445334} {:answer/id #uuid "7048ec9a-91f3-5e70-89ed-a45fb3e31905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$628$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445345} {:answer/id #uuid "789ebe1b-2dd1-5b2f-9475-7f0a0488da18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$620$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445346} {:answer/id #uuid "80e6d145-65a0-5b9c-af2c-46945fb281ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$612$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445349} {:answer/id #uuid "d123b220-d31c-5343-8d01-36cf77ecb21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$640$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445334} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a75644c-e1e3-5402-a410-adaf11ab9750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "490a86ce-e0bc-535b-a5a3-da17f507f78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335599" :question/problem "A high-speed printer in an office building has a maximum printing capacity of $640$ pages per minute; however, current production is below this maximum. Since the technician started monitoring, the printer produced $480$ and $560$ pages per minute at minutes $6$ and $12,$ respectively. What is the printer's printing rate at minute $36?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the printing production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in minutes.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the printing production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible printing production and its current printing production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the printer, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=640$ pages, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(640-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{640-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{640-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|640-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $640-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}640-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 640-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 640-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =640-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, at minute $6,$ the printing production was $480$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(6)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =480 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-6k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, at minute $12,$ the printing production was $560$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(12)=560,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(12) & =560 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-12k} & =560 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{160}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =2 \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-6\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} & =160 \\\\ K & =160{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\ K & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=640-320{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of pages printed per minute at minute $t=36$ is\n\n$$\nE(36)=640-320{e}^{-(36/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}=635\\,\\text{pages per minute}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(110-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445336} {:answer/id #uuid "207c4468-291f-59e8-87bb-de8bc6fba242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(30-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445338} {:answer/id #uuid "353017b2-4607-5104-afdb-932463c44124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(280-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445341} {:answer/id #uuid "83fdb903-a322-5109-85eb-f06a871c051d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(190-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445350} {:answer/id #uuid "c25a8b30-27a2-57da-864d-b562f2ea668b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(70-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445336} :answer-field/id #uuid "49c71e39-1e77-56e6-92ec-6a20e0c6c4b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "531996ff-0ee7-5943-af5b-93c7fc2f947e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335144" :question/problem "A bird sanctuary initially has $80$ birds. The number of birds in the sanctuary $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the sanctuary is $110$ birds, which differential equation could be used to model the bird population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of birds in the sanctuary obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of birds changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of birds in the sanctuary. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the bird sanctuary, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=110$ birds, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(110-B).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05356339-ef8c-55b3-b26e-1d5a396dcc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$19\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445332} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3494c8-d836-50c1-838b-c0be8c2b5c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$15\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445342} {:answer/id #uuid "8d11d60a-b68b-5f48-b3c9-db4c2154d10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$17\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445351} {:answer/id #uuid "99473357-017e-59bf-827f-4097bb32b024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445354} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc2a5b1-cdc4-5108-82ee-76eaa5f4a571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$22\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445332} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf03520-5f9c-50d4-9411-5e12d6f34453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "54a5418f-f9b3-5d4d-9779-13b8cf5ffccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335644" :question/problem "A coastal research station is monitoring the population of brine shrimp in a controlled seawater tank. The tank can sustain a maximum population of $500$ shrimp, but the population is still below this limit. Since researchers began tracking the growth, the population reached $452$ shrimp on day $3$ and $473$ shrimp on day $9.$ On what day will the population reach $490$ shrimp? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of shrimp, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the habitat can sustain, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500$ shrimp, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{500-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the population was $452$ shrimp. So, applying the condition $P(3)=452,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =452 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-3k} & =452 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the population was $473$ shrimp. So, applying the condition $P(9)=473,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =473 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-9k} & =473 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{48}{27} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{16}{9}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{16}{9}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)} & =48 \\\\ K & =48{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)} \\\\ K & =48\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ K & =48\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ K & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=500-64{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$ be the day when the population reaches $490$ shrimp. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =490 \\\\ 500-64{e}^{-kd} & =490 \\\\ 10 & =64{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{10}{64}=\\frac{5}{32} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{32}\\right) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{32}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $490$ shrimp on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{32}\\right)}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{16}{9}\\right)}=19.3578…≈19,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d3b80cf-1624-511b-9f84-0dbd23445796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.001P-25,\\,P(0)=3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445337} {:answer/id #uuid "26f02fa5-9d0e-5303-a3ab-ebbe45c96c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P,\\,P(0)=10\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445339} {:answer/id #uuid "e677655c-880b-5a45-8220-b2709155e399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=3-0.000\\,4P,\\,P(0)=13\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445368} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ae9662-9561-5fab-8fd8-0e95098b9508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P-225,\\,P(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445369} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8066cc-27f1-54d2-868f-a05b90f194ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=5-0.005P,\\,P(0)=2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445339} :answer-field/id #uuid "a035cca5-87cc-5312-bfcd-68835b076102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a1e7865-6d9a-51ad-a7cc-78b26889323e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15593" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P,\\,P(0)=10\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P=0.000\\,05(20\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.000\\,05,a=20\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=10\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=3-0.000\\,4P=0.000\\,4(7,500-P),\\,P(0)=13\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5-0.005P=0.005(1\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=2\\,000\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=13\\,000>a=7\\,500,$ and ${y}_{0}=2\\,000>a=1\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90bf424a-a5c7-51c0-9e49-9cf13b16339d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.3(y+200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445352} {:answer/id #uuid "b950d605-acf5-5f78-8ce1-1df43ba7eee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(200-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445359} {:answer/id #uuid "c44d4eaa-a7ce-5ab7-ae4e-afa08dff211b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=200(y+0.3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445362} {:answer/id #uuid "c636b844-1f99-53bf-a9e3-46fb747c0939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(y-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445363} {:answer/id #uuid "d1448329-aba0-5e4c-a382-514f67f96729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=200(y-0.3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445359} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b69f173-f151-5b59-a56f-2ab0651775c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9240a0d5-9357-59a5-be58-f932d93ba4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335028" :question/problem "Given that $y<200$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(200-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and $a=200.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<200$ for all $t\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e58d094-5b17-5bdb-b3cc-ac9ced48cf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.85P-346,\\,P(0)=451.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445335} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8720f5-820e-5963-b078-aeb673451966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=26-0.005P,\\,P(0)=5\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445355} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bed931-7bc1-5eaf-b7ae-e65ed3f624aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P,\\,P(0)=31\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445356} {:answer/id #uuid "f7934c18-cba3-5f1e-b7f5-2378dd7ae5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,216-8P,\\,P(0)=291" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445373} {:answer/id #uuid "fb732190-8be4-59f5-8e54-9b8dba6f50df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=436P-139\\,956,\\,P(0)=165\\,143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445356} :answer-field/id #uuid "074e4dbe-f5ba-52f9-92ce-1e5707e85955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d00e40d-1dde-5d1e-965b-4da23aadf13a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37332" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P,\\,P(0)=31\\,200,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P=0.02(31\\,900-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.02,a=31\\,900,$ and ${y}_{0}=31\\,200.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,216-8P=8(277-P),\\,P(0)=291,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=26-0.005P=0.005(5\\,200-P),\\,P(0)=5\\,600\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=291>a=277$ and ${y}_{0}=5\\,600>a=5\\,200.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445929} {:answer/id #uuid "4815316b-c603-51a2-a732-458284f7c774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y-90),\\,y(0)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445933} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1b5621-3f57-51c5-806e-e99b647b764b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445935} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e26874-dc7d-53de-91b5-91950a6ae0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445948} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fd2ab7-1578-5858-845a-20b1a7944785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d452b5a-2fc4-539e-b756-0fb969fcb4bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c85f83e-b227-560c-a6a8-ac2b41f8a28a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106656" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=5,a=90,$ and ${y}_{0}=130.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=60,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=60<a=90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445927} {:answer/id #uuid "11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445928} {:answer/id #uuid "7de9a5d8-fca6-54df-be6c-1bc3b035ed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445939} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f10bfc-7725-5838-829c-638f4fde25b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30y(y-200),\\,y(0)=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445942} {:answer/id #uuid "cddd42ed-edf0-54e7-998a-1f3cc455ccb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30y(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445928} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c16440-4f36-5799-80af-9e7a27fdee13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106658" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=30,a=200,$ and ${y}_{0}=210.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=100<a=200.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445932} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445934} {:answer/id #uuid "6056756e-9986-54df-b147-e00ce576b435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445936} {:answer/id #uuid "61ae82ec-9879-5f5d-ab92-a1997ae823f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445937} {:answer/id #uuid "f46e95ee-3654-530d-b86d-938504c88cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445934} :answer-field/id #uuid "08edadb3-2531-58ae-aec8-c90d8179de19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106932" :question/problem "The rate at which a cup of chocolate cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the chocolate in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-16).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=60{y}^{2}-960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445930} {:answer/id #uuid "3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445931} {:answer/id #uuid "c269d59b-4c24-5a2e-8b68-d56b249ca9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960+60y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445943} {:answer/id #uuid "c77bbc7e-9d55-561a-80a6-ebb8b2046097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445944} {:answer/id #uuid "fd079cb4-c93e-5568-a211-4982fc99b62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960y-60{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445931} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d8e051-9b26-56c6-8fc8-5f8fb9709d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106652" :question/problem "Given that $y>16$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-60(y-16).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=60,$ and $a=16.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>16$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$47\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485841} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0c7edc-dd85-5992-a102-fb9214299a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$40\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485952} {:answer/id #uuid "da50aece-8c74-5d03-baea-3fa39526eb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$56\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486160} {:answer/id #uuid "e16e1058-e495-57a8-9aff-1460d83a15f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$48\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486177} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdba2ac-27cc-530d-9112-686fd6e96177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$60\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485841} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbdf95f2-cd1f-5ed7-bee1-5be1370106ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0668c811-3c2b-52a1-8452-9d77f52aa1d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335473" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve is monitoring the population of a newly introduced species of deer. The habitat can sustain a maximum population of $850$ deer, but the herd is still below this limit. Since biologists began tracking the growth, the population reached $490$ deer on month $4$ and $600$ deer on month $12.$ On what month will the population reach $800$ deer? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of deer, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in months.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the farm, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=850$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(850-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{850-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{850-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|850-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $850-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}850-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 850-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 850-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =850-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on month $4,$ the population reached $490$ deer. So, applying the condition $P(4)=490,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =490 \\\\ 850-K{e}^{-4k} & =490 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =360.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on month $12,$ the population was $600$ deer. So, applying the condition $P(12)=600,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =600 \\\\ 850-K{e}^{-12k} & =600 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{360}{250} \\\\ {e}^{8k} & =\\frac{36}{25} \\\\ 8k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{36}{25}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{36}{25}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)} & =360 \\\\ K & =360{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)} \\\\ K & =360\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{25}} \\\\ K & =360\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ K & =432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=850-432{e}^{-(t/8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)}\n$$\n\nFinally, let $m$ be the month when the population reaches $800$ deer. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(m) & =800 \\\\ 850-432{e}^{-km} & =800 \\\\ 50 & =432{e}^{-km} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{50}{432} \\\\ -km & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{216}\\right) \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{216}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $500$ deer on month\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{216}\\right)}{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{36}{25}\\right)}=47.309…≈47,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04278896-0d0b-5abb-8627-033b3e542c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75+H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485774} {:answer/id #uuid "09aa1eb1-e4d1-5ba6-831f-ff5b8460e95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k{H}^{2}(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485785} {:answer/id #uuid "37ece5ef-defd-5270-8c15-ef93957859b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485872} {:answer/id #uuid "5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485914} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0c4047-f0a5-5bbe-9e6d-196692c6e874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485914} :answer-field/id #uuid "15815c67-c7dd-5da5-a708-0978463c7ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207" :question/math-academy-id "q-344215" :question/problem "A hospital administrator observed that the growth of the number of occupied beds in a hospital is directly proportional to the difference between the hospital’s maximum bed capacity and the current number of occupied beds. Let $H(t)$ be the number of occupied beds, measured in beds, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of beds the hospital can support is $75$ beds, which differential equation could be used to model the number of occupied beds in the hospital? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of occupied beds $H(t)$ in the hospital changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of beds the hospital can support and the current number of occupied beds. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-H),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of beds the hospital can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=75\\text{ beds},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4a4a2d-af4a-591d-a8c6-0601fcd81ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485843} {:answer/id #uuid "6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$17\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485932} {:answer/id #uuid "74c4a600-f1cd-550e-8301-06f89a755629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485965} {:answer/id #uuid "9e717efe-369e-59b8-9552-86a39b1b2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$22\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486048} {:answer/id #uuid "e38477fa-d2cc-596e-abba-962f5089409f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485932} :answer-field/id #uuid "9499f8ab-b442-53e2-8454-60e3c76d0bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "745cf184-0f4c-5376-bafe-668674640edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335649" :question/problem "A mountain valley is monitoring the population of a reintroduced elk herd. The valley can sustain a maximum population of $600$ elk, but the herd is still below this limit. Since ecologists began tracking the growth, the population reached $536$ elk on month $4$ and $573$ elk on month $10.$ On what month will the population reach $590$ elk? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of elk, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in months.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the valley can sustain, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ elk, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(600-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{600-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{600-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on month $4,$ the population was $536$ elk. So, applying the condition $P(4)=536,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =536 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =536 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on month $10,$ the population was $573$ elk. So, applying the condition $P(10)=573,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =573 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-10k} & =573 \\\\ K{e}^{-10k} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-10k}} & =\\frac{64}{27} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{64}{27} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{64}{27}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{64}{27}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} & =64 \\\\ K & =64{e}^{(4/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} \\\\ K & =64{e}^{(2/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} \\\\ K & =64{\\left(\\frac{64}{27}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\ K & =\\frac{1\\,024}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=600-\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $m$ be the month when the population reaches $590$ elk. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(m) & =590 \\\\ 600-\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-km} & =590 \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-km} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{90}{1\\,024} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{45}{512} \\\\ -km & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{45}{512}\\right) \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{45}{512}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $590$ elk on month\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{45}{512}\\right)}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{64}{27}\\right)}=16.905…≈17,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339a07cb-65ed-5553-99c1-8ac87a3d2893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{98}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486705} {:answer/id #uuid "43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{126}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486741} {:answer/id #uuid "9d515764-afcd-5111-817f-5442e35ae0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{112}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486947} {:answer/id #uuid "b82eed89-d5fa-5f72-8196-eb6c23acabba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486997} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9f3273-c38d-5046-8ba2-d45f97de8bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{134}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486741} :answer-field/id #uuid "3746981f-0e79-5e47-8df3-c9e044188fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107128" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of an exhaust pipe of a motorcycle can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe is ${140}^{∘}F,$ calculate its temperature after $3$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the exhaust pipe of a motorcycle is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-0.08\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-\\int 0.08\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-74| & =-0.08t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-74>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-74 & ={e}^{-0.08t+C} \\\\ T-74 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T-74 & =K{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T & =74+K{e}^{-0.08t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe was ${140}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =74+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =74+K \\\\ K & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=74+66{e}^{-0.08t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the exhaust pipe after $t=3$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(3)=74+66{e}^{-0.08\\cdot 3}≈{126}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fcd547-653b-552b-85ad-75b74c038726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488355} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d70a89-ba08-5cbb-ac39-03d367adfd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{5}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488367} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c8cd93-0c8d-5137-a955-741377209ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}ln⁡4+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488372} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7ec02e-0659-522a-9153-1d205b3645fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488374} {:answer/id #uuid "f7cf6924-5468-598a-89b2-51aedcf4589d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488355} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad71a46-de5e-5a86-86a7-aaf01c87103b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "06b0b1bd-ae5d-56cf-89fa-4d6392983b6e" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fdcf3f-cfff-57a7-b386-399774907aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{8}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488354} {:answer/id #uuid "2240439f-aa39-5a8b-a4e7-0445fac25c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{{8}^{x}}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488356} {:answer/id #uuid "3588b62e-52d0-5854-8cfb-d3eb9a170048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488360} {:answer/id #uuid "5c435c70-c370-5bfe-857a-8aa078242872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488362} {:answer/id #uuid "b1fd9c08-94f9-5e9c-a9a4-400afcf79210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89c7d86-b994-59e0-9505-ce73c2e35845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "12892343-09b2-531e-8c18-007ed29f4bb2" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({8}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({8}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {8}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{8}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+3{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ee3cd5-9bae-5d8a-9e2a-a4675e5a819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{{4}^{x}}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488351} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9e27d9-89f8-53e7-bcc7-4bcfa57e5a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488352} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddfbb88-44a7-5bb9-8067-0a62e7d696fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488358} {:answer/id #uuid "ca214c2d-db08-5860-8467-809d7c49fb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488370} {:answer/id #uuid "f692703e-3baa-546c-becb-b363894630f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488352} :answer-field/id #uuid "311ca5eb-63f3-578f-bd8d-a1d406692c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98ccc59f-c7a3-5907-b514-b90395053cf2" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({4}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({4}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+3{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aea10bb-853d-516c-8881-38e0458d2aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-\\frac{{7}^{2x}}{ln⁡x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488357} {:answer/id #uuid "68c0b390-e877-55ee-9e76-30d056d7e23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{7}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488363} {:answer/id #uuid "c779612a-03b7-5b03-bdd5-d469206d5055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{7}^{x}}{ln⁡7}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488368} {:answer/id #uuid "e3714c2f-038a-502c-8e59-d26f59c829cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488371} {:answer/id #uuid "e862b7ff-3d80-5b2f-b753-3257f1333194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{7}^{x}}{ln⁡7}+{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488368} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c4d617-33df-5106-88ff-53415e61c0a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f83b5753-3204-51de-9a63-cf67f502215f" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{e}^{x}-{7}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{7}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{7}^{x}}{ln⁡7}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1f60e01-bcaa-538a-85d1-4c41aee48064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20(4x+6)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493335} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8870fe-f483-4a24-aa31-063adbde72e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\left(4x+6\\right)^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493335} :answer-field/id #uuid "74945d2f-3779-5856-8f0b-b1fe26940f82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c698fb8-2f47-52aa-ab20-d02b17699d11" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+6)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+6$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+6{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+6{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6b327a-95b5-56cb-b8b1-80852501eb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493327} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a534b2c-8471-58d8-901e-8ca326167938" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "97e8ab80-425a-5ce3-aeca-1b6c32080f6d" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{5x-3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=5x-3$ and $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{g(x)}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\sqrt{g})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(5x-3)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df5e2ea6-5eec-545e-bf02-640b9eb005aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493334} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9beb5af-bf72-501b-8c38-28d625a4d182" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a645231c-c79c-561b-9b41-b96c53332e19" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{5}-7{x}^{4}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})^{′}=5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})(\\,5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0faa8321-e556-549e-8043-4491148d0785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9f93b13c0c010da3a78b5bbd76d5fef33f5ad73ee3c2dfde2de14cfaab61b758.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496393} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab31702-2a56-5fe8-9619-670c0f45ebbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/18/18bfe4ae3bbd0e3897efba5da0b744999da4deb8bf116820710bb6f74e452e44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496400} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ac49bb-68d7-5306-81a1-458e043568c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/63/634d354bffd59865b55d905e44df53cf0f3b12e2f47e93ee9862d5ffcddcfe91.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496420} {:answer/id #uuid "b847c212-055e-58e0-b502-e6968aa2c28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e6/e662867dfd408805fd1f750b20190337db32abf983d43c93cf8c90a243726a7f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496422} {:answer/id #uuid "eeed2a8f-405d-508f-a36d-360a5782b32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c0/c0df217b6b80cfc2791ac063a907e4ad23096de278e1ec1ce2a417f2b5cd81fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496393} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d95b6a-70bd-53d9-b60a-4df910d6ad89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c56f44e-ca61-59ca-ab47-41b9727c4f9c" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =3\\int \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{\\left(x - 2\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =\\left(x - 2\\right)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a2609542e1b48653ea5d167bb34d62f8b4ddefe3412b2a3c8804b7b50284fab.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a3ecd7-a741-5deb-abdc-1ecdb08d7f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a1/a1436e6273de88dd0f122f0142dd3157db2ec007fb7670aaa555969bbb7adeed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496396} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c04fb-8750-5cb5-b025-5eb00c605645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e7/e76046e12180d9892dacb64c0c2669ced875271819e756f822550f10fb77fb45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496413} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecdde9b-858a-5b1b-8851-f76f0b3930d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/84/84bf7d020c65bebbe23fb8d2dd7e919a3a1ac2ab27978c91cbe89973085aeb43.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496415} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3bfefe-920a-5618-a806-1b47b244864b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fee181bd159fe9d1fbe615bed1ef9b7eddc424342d473432725d468ac9df6d5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496426} {:answer/id #uuid "f747cbad-15f5-5530-ade3-1e452e56a875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bb/bb8a9db0e8d8c91c6f4296b21aef84a242b989b4257e3cef3282adf5174ed079.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496396} :answer-field/id #uuid "59fa873c-1ad6-5952-be17-ba65dbd1e41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "408bf66a-2a8e-5c7e-aa05-3b6725a15b53" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{9 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}}{2}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- \\frac{9 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\int \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{3 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{3}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/f0/f0abc54ee55826263306f014480e9a4d36c4d0d3fd10b4c51757d03f38d00ae6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4969ec6c-e9f4-588d-8add-22b688d7f5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/df/dfde7e57d4f1ccff20d93f1d8144e0b3df5831d7be121bce5911582cdc360c79.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496408} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb5e34e-5cad-5d3e-bffd-d4db835005ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a0/a083eb2f43215be1f455b